   693	6 The arithmetic progression version of the conjecture
   694	The formally strongest version of the Strong(α,β)-Divisor Conjecture is that there are
   695	arithmetic progressionsAof cardinalityn 2β, whose elements have magnitudeexp(nα), that
   696	satisfy then-divisor property. We call this the “Arithmetic Progression Version” of the
   697	conjecture. Proposition 3.4 shows that the Arithmetic Progression Version implies the Strong
   698	(α,β)-Divisor Conjecture. Thus, if one is to disprove a version of the conjecture, the easiest
   699	version to attack is the arithmetic progression one. Here we make some observations that
   700	may be useful in studying this version.
   701	First, recall that such an arithmetic progressionA={b+ic: 1≤i≤n 2β}satisfying the
   702	n-Divisor Property yields an improved exponent for polynomial factorization ifα,β <1/2.
   703	One observation is thatb̸= 0is necessary for non-triviality. For ifb= 0then we have that
   704	every primep < ndividesicfor somei≤n 2β, and this requirescto be the product of all
   705	primes betweenn 2β andn, which is a number of magnitudeexp(n−o(1))whenβ <1/2.
   706	It is also necessary that(b+c)≥exp(n 1−2β)as we have argued in Section 3. Although it
   707	seems unlikely, we cannot rule out the possibility that the Arithmetic Progression Version
   708	may hold withb≤exp(O(n 1−2β))andc= 1!
   709	Certainsubsetsof integers in[n]are easy to handle: for example, by choosingcto be the
   710	least common multiple of all positive integers of magnitude at mostnα, andb= 0, it is easy
   711	to see that the arithmetic progressionA={b+ic: 1≤i≤n 2β}contains multiples of each
   712	integer that can be writtenxywherex≤n 2β andy≤n α. On the other hand, some subsets
   713	of integers seem difficult. Two challenging sets of integers are (1) the primes in[n], and (2)
   714	compositesxywherex,yare in the range[n 1/2−ϵ,n 1/2]for a constantϵ>0.
   715	A useful fact is that different arithmetic progressions containing multiples of different
   716	subsets of integers can be combined into a single generalized arithmetic progression:
   717	Lemma 6.1.LetA 1 ={b 1 +ic 1 : 1≤i≤ℓ 1}andA 2 ={b 2 +ic 2 : 1≤i≤ℓ 2}be two
   718	arithmetic progressions. Then the generalized arithmetic progression
   719	A={c 1b2 +i(c 1c2) +j(c 2b1−c 1b2) : 1≤i≤max(ℓ 1,ℓ 2),0≤j≤1}
   720	contains multiples ofxifA 1 orA 2 does.
   721	Proof.Supposexdividesb 1 +ic 1 for somei. Then the(i,1)term inA, which equals
   722	c2b1 +i(c 1c2) =c 2(b1 +ic 1)is also divisible byx. Similarly, ifxdividesb 2 +ic 2 for somei,
   723	then the(i,0)term inA, which equalsc 1b2 +i(c 1c2) =c 1(b2 +ic 2)is divisible byx.
