   800	7 Conclusions and open problems
   801	It is certainly possible that the Arithmetic Progression Version of the Strong(α,β)-Divisor
   802	Conjecture is false, and the same could be true for any or all of the other variants. Even
   803	though some variants of the conjecture may turn out to be false, we note that there is still
   804	room for the ideas and algorithmic machinery in this paper to lead to improved algorithms
   805	for factoring polynomials and integers. This is because certain generalizations of the main
   806	conjectures are possible, and these generalizations could be true even if the versions we
   807	21
   808	=== PAGE 23 ===
   809	have discussed here are false. For example, one can replace the requirement thatS,Tare
   810	generalized arithmetic progressions with the more general requirement that they have short
   811	addition sequences. This generalization of the classicaddition chainswas introduced by
   812	Knuth in [Knu97] (§4.6.3problem32) and by Downey, Leong and Sethi in [DLS81], and
   813	some nontrivial results are known in [DLS81] and [DL80]. Addition sequences are sufficient
   814	because Lemma 4.2 remains true ifS,Thave short addition sequences, with generalized
   815	arithmetic progressions being just one example of a suitably short addition sequence.
   816	Beyond directly proving or refuting the main conjectures, we highlight the case mentioned
   817	in Section 6, of finding a length-n2β arithmetic progression consisting of integers of magnitude
   818	exp(nα)that contains multiples of the compositesxywithx,yin the range[n 1/2−ϵ,n 1/2], for
   819	a constantϵ >0. Perhaps one can find a characterization similar to Lemma 6.2 for this
   820	subset of integers. Alternatively, can one give a reduction to the “prime case” discussed in
   821	Lemma 6.2? It would be interesting to establish that the prime case is the “hardest” case
   822	formally.
   823	On the algorithmic side, we wonder whether a deterministic version of Theorem 4.8 is
   824	possible. This would make our overall algorithm for distinct-degree factorization determin-
   825	istic; the other algorithms (including the best-known exponent3/2one) for this problem
   826	are deterministic. We still wouldn’t obtain fast deterministic algorithms for polynomial fac-
   827	torization, because the known equal-degree factorization algorithms are randomized, but
   828	derandomizing Theorem 4.8 is still an interesting challenge by itself.
   829	Finally, it remains open to find a nearly-linear time algorithm for univariate polynomial
   830	factorization over a finite field, with or without the number-theoretic conjecture, which can
   831	prove exponent 4/3 at best.
