    20	Consequently, the arithmetic-progression version is false whenever α < 1/2 and β < 3/8.
    21	In particular, it is false at the proposed point ( α, β) = (1/3, 1/3), even if both bounds are
    22	relaxed by no(1) at the exponent level. The proof uses primes in a fixed band below √n to
    23	turn semiprime divisibility into a finite incidence structure. An elementary bounded-degree
    24	linear-space estimate then gives the result. This theorem concerns the one-dimensional
    25	arithmetic-progression version only; it does not disprove the higher-rank Strong Divisor
    26	Conjecture.
    27	1 Introduction and statement of results
    28	For n∈Z >0, a finite set A⊂Z >0 has the n-divisor propertyif every d∈ {1, . . . , n}divides at
    29	least one member of A. Umans and Wang introduced conjectures asking for n-divisor sets with
    30	additional difference-set structure and small height [ 4]. Proposition 3.4 and Section 6 of their
    31	paper single out a formally stronger sufficient statement: for infinitely many n, there should be a
    32	positive arithmetic progression
    33	A={b+ic: 0≤i < L}, L≤n 2β,
    34	whose members have magnitude at most exp(nα) and which has the n-divisor property. They
    35	call this theArithmetic Progression Versionof the Strong ( α, β)-Divisor Conjecture. It is an
    36	informally named version rather than a separately numbered conjecture.
    37	Our main theorem gives a quantitative obstruction to every such progression of sub-square-root
    38	logarithmic height.
