   227	Remark4.1 (Positivity and zero).The n-divisor property in Definition 3.1 of Umans and Wang
   228	is explicitly defined for sets of positive integers. Thus zero is not an admissible progression term
   229	or divisor witness here. Allowing zero as a witness would make the condition vacuous, since every
   230	positive integer divides zero. A constant progression reduces to the singleton case treated in the
   231	proof.
   232	Remark4.2 (What has and has not been disproved).Proposition 3.4 of Umans and Wang [ 4] shows
   233	that the Arithmetic Progression Version implies the Strong ( α, β)-Divisor Conjecture; no converse
   234	is asserted. The result above consequently doesnotdisprove the full Strong (1 /3, 1/3)-Divisor
   235	Conjecture. A difference of two higher-rank generalized arithmetic progressions need not be a
   236	one-dimensional progression. The decisive step above uses
   237	(u+ic)−(u+jc) = (i−j)c.
   238	For a rank-two progression, the corresponding difference contains two independent coefficients,
   239	and the at-most-one-intersection argument does not follow.
   240	Remark4.3 (Endpoints).Theorem 1.1 does not cover the case logH = O(√n), and Corollary 1.2
   241	makes no claim when β = 3 /8. Indeed, the lower bound L≫n 3/4/√logn does not exclude
   242	L≤n 3/4. The constant p8/27 is the outcome of the fixed-band argument and is not claimed to
