   562	5 Using the conjecture to factor integers
   563	In this section, we show that essentially the same ideas as used in Section 4 can be used
   564	to give deterministic factoring algorithms for integers. In this regime, the state-of-the-art
   565	algorithms are exponential algorithms with running time of the form~O(Nγ)for0<γ <1,
   566	whereNis the integer to be factored. This contrasts with randomized integer factoring which
   567	is heuristicallyexp( ~O(logγN))time (e.g. the Number Field Sieve). The trivial trial division
   568	method has running time~O(N 1/2), and classic results of Pollard [Pol74] and Strassen [Str77]
   569	give ~O(N 1/4). Recent work has improved the exponent from1/4to1/5using additional
   570	ideas [Har21]. If a version of our strong(α,β)-Divisor Conjecture is true forα,β= 1/3,
   571	we show that this improves the exponent further to1/6, and would give a deterministic
   572	algorithm with the best known running time.
   573	In order to apply the conjecture, we need to assume an additional property. It is likely
   574	that if the conjecture is true, the construction of the structured setsS,Twould be done
   575	in such a way that the different divisors in then-Divisor property would be allocated to
   576	the variouss−tdifferences in a prescribed or “known” fashion. In this case it is plausible
   577	to expect an efficient deterministic function which lists the prime factors of a givens−t
   578	difference, in time ~O(nα). For ease of exposition, we give a name to the version of the
   579	conjecture which presumes the existence of such a function, as follows:
   580	15
   581	=== PAGE 17 ===
   582	Conjecture 5.1.(Strong Prefactored(α,β)-Divisor Conjecture) For infinitely manyn,
   583	there exists subsetsS,T⊆Z + that satisfy the requirements of Conjecture 3.3. Moreover, for
   584	every integers−twiths∈S,t∈T, its prime factorization can be output in deterministic
   585	time ~O(nα).
   586	As we will see, if the Strong Prefactored(α,β)-Divisor Conjecture holds forα,β <2/5
   587	then there exists a determinstic algorithm to factor integers that beats the current-best
   588	exponent of1/5; if the conjecture holds forα,β= 1/3(which is best possible), then the
   589	exponent is1/6.
